.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt BIO_S_FD 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm BIO_s_fd ,
.Nm BIO_set_fd ,
.Nm BIO_get_fd ,
.Nm BIO_new_fd
.Nd file descriptor BIO
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/bio.h
.Ft const BIO_METHOD *
.Fo BIO_s_fd
.Fa void
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo BIO_set_fd
.Fa "BIO *b"
.Fa "int fd"
.Fa "int c"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo BIO_get_fd
.Fa "BIO *b"
.Fa "int *c"
.Fc
.Ft BIO *
.Fo BIO_new_fd
.Fa "int fd"
.Fa "int close_flag"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn BIO_s_fd
returns the file descriptor BIO method.
This is a wrapper round the platforms file descriptor routines such as
.Xr read 3
and
.Xr write 3 .
.Pp
.Xr BIO_read_ex 3
and
.Xr BIO_write_ex 3
read or write the underlying descriptor.
.Xr BIO_puts 3
is supported but
.Xr BIO_gets 3
is not.
.Pp
If the close flag is set then
.Xr close 3
is called on the underlying file descriptor when the BIO is freed.
.Pp
.Xr BIO_reset 3
attempts to change the file pointer to the start of file such as by
using
.Sy lseek(fd, 0, 0) .
.Pp
.Xr BIO_seek 3
sets the file pointer to position
.Sy ofs
from start of file such as by using
.Sy lseek(fd, ofs, 0) .
.Pp
.Xr BIO_tell 3
returns the current file position such as by calling
.Sy lseek(fd, 0, 1) .
.Pp
.Fn BIO_set_fd
sets the file descriptor of BIO
.Fa b
to
.Fa fd
and the close flag to
.Fa c .
.Pp
.Fn BIO_get_fd
places the file descriptor of BIO
.Fa b
in
.Fa c
if it is not NULL. It also returns the file descriptor.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_new_fd
returns a file descriptor BIO using
.Fa fd
and
.Fa close_flag .
.Sh NOTES
The behaviour of
.Xr BIO_read_ex 3
and
.Xr BIO_write_ex 3
depends on the behavior of the platforms
.Xr read 3
and
.Xr write 3
calls on the descriptor.
If the underlying file descriptor is in a non blocking mode then the BIO
will behave in the manner described in the
.Xr BIO_read_ex 3
and
.Xr BIO_should_retry 3
manual pages.
.Pp
File descriptor BIOs should not be used for socket I/O. Use socket BIOs
instead.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_set_fd
and
.Fn BIO_get_fd
are implemented as macros.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn BIO_s_fd
returns the file descriptor BIO method.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_set_fd
returns 1 on success or <=0 for failure.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_get_fd
returns the file descriptor or -1 if the BIO has not been initialized.
It also returns zero and negative values if other error occurs.
.Pp
.Fn BIO_new_fd
returns the newly allocated BIO or NULL is an error occurred.
.Sh EXAMPLES
This is a file descriptor BIO version of "Hello World":
.Bd -literal
 BIO *out;

 out = BIO_new_fd(fileno(stdout), BIO_NOCLOSE);
 BIO_printf(out, "Hello World\en");
 BIO_free(out);
.Ed
